nvgpu: gm20b: small pages when the GPU has no IOMMU

The Nano's device tree for 5.10 leaves out NVIDIA's downstream SMMU, which
5.10 has no driver for, so the GPU uses direct DMA. nvgpu's big pages need
contiguous I/O addresses, which direct DMA cannot promise. Turn big pages
off on gm20b when the GPU has no IOMMU domain, as R35's gv11b platform
already does.

Tested on the Nano: CUDA 10.2 passed every test under 5.10 in trial 11.

Licence: GPL-2.0-only. Lab notes, with every measurement: NOTES.md.
---
diff --git a/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c b/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c
index de6ac5b78..504c51ac7 100644
--- a/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c
+++ b/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include <uapi/linux/nvgpu.h>
 #include <linux/dma-buf.h>
 #include <linux/reset.h>
+#include <linux/iommu.h>
 #include <nvgpu/vpr.h>
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_TEGRA_DVFS)
@@ -890,6 +891,12 @@ static int gk20a_tegra_probe(struct device *dev)
 		return ret;
 #endif
 
+	/*
+	 * Big pages need contiguous IOVA from the SMMU. Without an IOMMU
+	 * domain, map with small pages, as the gv11b platform does.
+	 */
+	platform->disable_bigpage = !iommu_get_domain_for_dev(dev);
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_OF
 	of_chosen = of_find_node_by_path("/chosen");
 	if (!of_chosen)
